A: In this test, the assumption of the 'ArrayList' parameter is being made in the "PUT_RemoveContract" method where the test is being observed, and it's being considered as an actual value with some examples of input values. During your test run, these assumptions are being used for providing examples in the test explorer. Therefore, the test itself may not generate all values uniformly i.e., for all different inputs in your actual run which is why the assertion failed.

You may try to create another [TestMethod] where you would provide different inputs explicitly instead of assumption. Just provide some different values that you know will cause the failure. 

Also note that [PexAssumeUnderTest] is used to specify which method signature the data is generated (the PexAssumeUnderTest must be on the method, not on the argument). So, it doesn't matter the value of 'x' as the method being tested is 'Remove', not 'Remove(x)'. Thus, there is really no 'x' in the test under any circumstances.

If you cannot make it work, you may use other mutation testing tools - they can generate a wider set of test values. They generate much larger test data than traditional unit testing with assumptions, often used in practice to stress-test code mutations. 

Lastly, make sure that all your test data is generated from an acceptable domain. If you have a domain like Natural Numbers (only positive integers), mutation testing tools might not generate any test cases that cover edge cases (like 0 or negative numbers). Again, your problem should not happen under these scenarios, so those test cases would fail. Make sure your test domain covers all edge cases. And again, this is purely based on assumptions in a test, and they don't necessarily cover all real situations.
In general, mutation testing, or any other form of randomized testing, is always a great tool in the arsenal of a tester. It can help to find edge cases where your tests may fail, covering the untested areas of your code bases effectively. It may not replace the need for comprehensive test coverage, but could definitely provide a boost to increase the coverage significantly.
Sorry for the confusion and thanks for your patience.

