## PexObserve
The Microsoft PexObserve class allows developers to attach auxiliary data to a test method for easy exploration during the debugging process. The data attached can include the values of variables, returned values, or even the return type of a method.

In the ArrayList test method PUT_RemoveContract, PexObserve is used to record the Count, IndexOf, LastIndexOf, and Contains properties of the original and new ArrayList state.

Key: $old_arrList_Count, $new_arrList_Count, $old_arrList_IndexOf_x, $new_arrList_IndexOf_x, $old_arrList_LastIndexOf_x, $new_arrList_LastIndexOf_x, $old_arrList_contains_x, $new_arrList_contains_x.

Value: the current value of the data being recorded.


******
## Debug.Assert
These are used to specify the condition that should be true or false. If the statement doesn't hold true, then the program will halt its execution and notify the debugger.

The assertions are checking the index values and count.

1. An IndexOf x in new ArrayList should be -1 or positive (index >=0) to ensure it cannot find the removed element in the new ArrayList. This checks whether the item was successfully removed.

2. The LastIndexOf x in the new ArrayList should also be -1 or positive. This verifies that the item was not found anywhere else in the Array after it was removed, indicating it may not have been present in the first case.

3. The count in the old ArrayList should be greater than the count in the new ArrayList, confirming that an item was removed and therefore the count was decreased.


******
## AssumePrecondition
The AssumePrecondition class is used to determine the preconditions of a function or operation in the code.

Here it is used in the Remove operation to confirm that the condition is met for testing and avoid potential null exceptions.
******
## Conclusion
The provided test case demonstrates how a PexLab test script can be created using visual studio TestTools.UnitTesting framework. The test validates the functionality of the ArrayList's Remove method. The script runs all the methods under an assumed precondition and observes the effect of removing an item on the ArrayList, checking for correctness using the Assert and Assume classes, along with observing essential data points for debugging purposes using the PexObserve class. The test covers possible values for all the tested ArrayList methods, including its Count, IndexOf, LastIndexOf, and Contains properties, and verifies the correctness of removal operations. This is an interesting demonstration of a test script for a complex method with a variety of potential inputs and outcomes.******

# Notes for Testing Further

1. The script should be tested against other ArrayList operations to comprehensively test the functionality of the ArrayList class as a whole.
2. It's also important to further verify the ArrayList class for different types of inputs and edge cases.
3. The test should also be executed in a setting where the target build or framework version may cause unexpected behaviours, to ensure that it works properly in the intended execution environment.--------------------            Assert.AreEqual<int>(-1, New_arrListIndexOfX);
	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ last index of x in new array is geq minus 1 
            Assert.AreEqual<int>(-1, New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ new array does not contain x 
            Assert.IsFalse(New_arrListContainsX);
	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ new array count is one less than old array count 
            Assert.AreEqual<int>(Old_arrListCount - 1, New_arrListCount);
        }
    }
}

//test/Microsoft.Pex.Framework/Samples/ArrayList/ArrayListContractTest.cs
﻿using System;
using System.Collections;
using System.Diagnostics.Contracts;
using Microsoft.Pex.Framework.Contracts;

[ContractClass(typeof(ArrayListContract))]
public interface IArrayListContractTest : IList {}

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void PUT_RemoveContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_x = x;
            int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            arrList.Remove(x);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_x", Old_x);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_IndexOf_x", Old_arrListIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_IndexOf_x", New_arrListIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_contains_x", Old_arrListContainsX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_contains_x", New_arrListContainsX);

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ index of x in new array is geq minus 1 
            Assert.AreEqual<int>(-1, New_arrListIndexOfX);
	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ last index of x in new array is geq minus 1 
            Assert.AreEqual<int>(-1, New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ new array does not contain x 
            Assert.IsFalse(New_arrListContainsX);
	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ new array count is one less than old array count 
            Assert.AreEqual<int>(Old_arrListCount - 1, New_arrListCount);
        }
    }
}

