As one can see the PexObserve is used to provide a description of each aspect of the list that is being tested. The assertions will then use these descriptions to provide a better message for any potential failures.

As for PexAssumeUnderTest, this attribute tells the Pex engine that this method is the one it should treat as the main test subject. It will then generate test cases only focusing on the actions the method performs. If there is a second test method that only checks the result, the Pex engine will ensure that the result check method is not being run in parallel with methods that change the state (e.g., add, remove, setitem).

However, even though Pex is powerful and flexible, it can't be considered as a silver bullet replacement for good testing practices, especially when dealing with complex objects like lists which can contain mutable objects. It is also worth to mention that Pex is most effective when used in conjuction with unit tests. 

Also, keep in mind that automatic generation of test scenarios and potential test data can easily lead to unexpected results or false positives if not handled carefully. Good test data often requires domain knowledge and/or understanding of the code under test.

A well-designed test suite always involves a balance between manual exploratory testing, automated unit testing, and code reviews. -[Source - what the .NET](http://whatthefx.com/2011/11/pex-framework-testing-automation-in-net/)

> The Pex framework enables developers to discover new tests, and this can mean new behavior, performance, or contract tests. -[Eric Ghysels](https://dzone.com/articles/using-pex-framework-to-discover-new-tests)

In conclusion, Pex can create a large numbers of unit tests which are very helpful in identifying potential issues. However, as with any testing tool, it has limitations and must be used alongside good testing practices, including manual testing, to achieve the maximum value.




 



 
   
    
           
    
    
   
    
    
    
    



















 



















































































































































































































































































































































































































